From: brainux-pw-a9300
Subject: [PATCH] Input: brain-kbd-i2c: read the keys in the IRQ thread

The driver passes its handler to devm_request_threaded_irq() as the
primary (hard IRQ) handler and NULL as the thread function, so the
handler runs in hard IRQ context. It reads the key codes with
i2c_smbus_read_word_swapped(), which takes the adapter's bus lock and
waits for the transfer. When another device on the same I2C bus is in
the middle of a transfer, this sleeps in atomic context:

  BUG: scheduling while atomic
  ... i2c_smbus_xfer <- i2c_smbus_read_word_data <- bk_i2c_irq_handler
  <- ... mxs_gpio_irq_handler

On PW-A9300 this happened while keys were auto-repeating and the audio
codec on I2C0 was being powered up/down by opening/closing the PCM.
The kernel survived, but the irq thread of the main LCD touch panel
(sx8650) oopsed and exited, so the touch panel stopped until reboot.

Pass the handler as the thread function, as IRQF_ONESHOT already
expects and as other I2C input drivers do (tca8418, sx8654).

diff --git a/drivers/input/keyboard/brain-kbd-i2c.c b/drivers/input/keyboard/brain-kbd-i2c.c
index 6f07c7a..a83f2c2 100644
--- a/drivers/input/keyboard/brain-kbd-i2c.c
+++ b/drivers/input/keyboard/brain-kbd-i2c.c
@@ -440,8 +440,9 @@ static int bk_i2c_probe(struct i2c_client *cli, const struct i2c_device_id *id)
 
 	i2c_smbus_read_word_data(kbd->cli, 0x04);
 
-	err = devm_request_threaded_irq(&cli->dev, cli->irq, bk_i2c_irq_handler,
-					NULL, IRQF_ONESHOT,
+	/* The handler reads over I2C, which may sleep: run it in the IRQ thread */
+	err = devm_request_threaded_irq(&cli->dev, cli->irq,
+					NULL, bk_i2c_irq_handler, IRQF_ONESHOT,
 					BRAIN_KBD_I2C_DEV_NAME, kbd);
 	if (err) {
 		dev_err(&cli->dev, "failed to request irq: %d\n", err);
